  <h1>Famous TV Shows of 2026</h1>

  <p class="subtitle">
    A record of the television shows that people watched, discussed,
    streamed, shared, remembered, and waited for during 2026.
  </p>

  <div class="metadata">
    Published September 4, 2026 · Looking at television in 2026
  </div>

</header>


<section>

  <p class="intro">
    Television in 2026 was no longer defined by a television set.
  </p>

  <p>
    People watched television shows on televisions, computers, phones,
    tablets, and other connected devices. Some watched episodes immediately
    when they were released. Others discovered shows years after they first
    aired.
  </p>

  <p>
    A television show could be a traditional broadcast series, a streaming
    original, a reality program, a cartoon, a children's program, a
    documentary, a limited series, or an older show that had suddenly found
    a new audience.
  </p>

  <p>
    This article records the shows that helped define that landscape.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not a ranking of the "best" television shows of 2026. Instead, it
    is a record of the shows that were visible in the culture of the year —
    whether because millions of people watched them, because they became
    important online, because they represented a major franchise, because
    they developed devoted communities, or simply because people kept
    talking about them.
  </p>

</section>


<section>

  <h2>How Television Was Measured in 2026</h2>

  <p>
    Measuring television became considerably more complicated once streaming
    became a major part of everyday viewing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Traditional television was often measured through audiences watching
    programs when they were broadcast. Streaming services introduced
    different measurements, including minutes watched, views, completion,
    subscribers, and other platform-specific statistics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nielsen's streaming measurements provide one of the most useful
    comparable records for U.S. viewing. Its first-half 2026 report measured
    streaming viewing in minutes and found that the five largest overall
    titles combined for 108 billion minutes. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Important:</strong> Viewing figures in this article are not
    necessarily worldwide totals. Different services publish different
    measurements, and many companies do not release comparable numbers.
    Where reliable figures exist, they are identified by their measurement
    type.
  </div>

</section>


<section>

  <h2>20 Shows That Defined 2026</h2>

  <p>
